      "text": "To find the positive value of \\( t \\) for which \\( |{-4 + ti}| = 2\\sqrt{13} \\), we need to understand the magnitude (or modulus) of a complex number \\( a + bi \\).\n\nThe modulus of the complex number \\( -4 + ti \\) is given by:\n\n\\[ |{-4 + ti}| = \\sqrt{a^2 + b^2} \\]\n\nHere, \\( a = -4 \\) and \\( b = t \\). So, the modulus becomes:\n\n\\[ |{-4 + ti}| = \\sqrt{(-4)^2 + t^2} = \\sqrt{16 + t^2} \\]\n\nWe are given that this modulus is equal to \\( 2\\sqrt{13} \\):\n\n\\[ \\sqrt{16 + t^2} = 2\\sqrt{13} \\]\n\nSquaring both sides of the equation to eliminate the square root:\n\n\\[ 16 + t^2 = (2\\sqrt{13})^2 \\]\n\nSimplifying \\((2\\sqrt{13})^2\\):\n\n\\[ 16 + t^2 = 4 \\cdot 13 \\]\n\\[ 16 + t^2 = 52 \\]\n\nSubtract 16 from both sides to isolate \\( t^2 \\):\n\n\\[ t^2 = 52 - 16 \\]\n\\[ t^2 = 36 \\]\n\nTaking the square root of both sides to solve for \\( t \\):\n\n\\[ t = \\pm \\sqrt{36} \\]\n\\[ t = \\pm 6 \\]\n\nSince we are asked for the positive value of \\( t \\):\n\n\\[ t = 6 \\]\n\nTherefore, the positive value of \\( t \\) satisfying the given condition is:\n\n\\(\\boxed{6}\\)",
