      "text": "To solve for the positive value of \\( t \\) that satisfies \\( |{-4 + ti}| = 2\\sqrt{13} \\), we need to use the properties of the modulus of a complex number.\n\nThe modulus of a complex number \\( a + bi \\) is given by:\n\\[ |a + bi| = \\sqrt{a^2 + b^2} \\]\n\nIn this problem, the complex number is \\( -4 + ti \\). Therefore:\n\\[ |{-4 + ti}| = \\sqrt{(-4)^2 + t^2} \\]\n\nGiven that this modulus is equal to \\( 2\\sqrt{13} \\), we can set up the following equation:\n\\[ \\sqrt{(-4)^2 + t^2} = 2\\sqrt{13} \\]\n\nFirst, simplify the expression inside the square root:\n\\[ \\sqrt{(-4)^2 + t^2} = \\sqrt{16 + t^2} \\]\n\nSince this equals \\( 2\\sqrt{13} \\), we can set the two expressions equal to each other:\n\\[ \\sqrt{16 + t^2} = 2\\sqrt{13} \\]\n\nNext, square both sides to eliminate the square root:\n\\[ (\\sqrt{16 + t^2})^2 = (2\\sqrt{13})^2 \\]\n\\[ 16 + t^2 = 4 \\times 13 \\]\n\\[ 16 + t^2 = 52 \\]\n\nSubtract 16 from both sides to isolate \\( t^2 \\):\n\\[ t^2 = 52 - 16 \\]\n\\[ t^2 = 36 \\]\n\nTake the positive square root to solve for \\( t \\) since we are asked for the positive value:\n\\[ t = \\sqrt{36} \\]\n\\[ t = 6 \\]\n\nThus, the positive value of \\( t \\) is \\( \\boxed{6} \\).",
